# The auxiliary build trees README.md documents.  The coverage and sanitizer
# recipes each configure a tree of their OWN -- cmake -B build-cov and
# cmake -B build-san -- precisely so the mandated flagless command stays
# uninstrumented, which means running either recipe as written leaves a
# directory beside build/ that the pattern above does not cover.  The two names
# the README uses are listed explicitly, and build-*/ covers any other tree made
# with that same -B build-<purpose> idiom.  Nothing tracked begins with "build":
# the only tracked path containing the word is tests/mock_core.h, which is
# matched by none of these.
build-cov/
build-san/
build-*/

# CTest's JUnit report.  ctest --output-junit resolves its argument relative to
# the build directory, so the README's own invocation lands inside build/ and is
# already covered -- but the same option run from the source tree, or given a
# path outside the build tree, is not.  The README names this exact filename, so
# it is named here too.
results.xml

# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED: core images.  Both fork sites in the suite --
# param_util.h's guarded-boundary harness and tests/test_err_death.c's abort
# harness -- drop RLIMIT_CORE to zero in the child before anything can fault, so
# an EXPECTED death writes no image and there is nothing to hide.  An
# unexpected one is a defect worth noticing in git status, and ignoring it would
# hide the regression rather than prevent it.  A pattern would also have to be
# written carefully: a glob such as *core* would match tests/mock_core.h.
